{
  "dom_id": "step-q14144",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "For the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{8}-18{x}^{2}-33x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $3,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 3.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{18}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm 3, \\\\ \\pm 18, & \\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)",
